<article class="rag-story" aria-label="An interactive introduction to retrieval augmented generation">
  <header class="story-hero">
    <span class="story-kicker">FIELD NOTE · RAG SYSTEMS</span>
    <h2>How an AI system learns to answer from the right evidence.</h2>
    <p class="story-dek">A gentle, interactive walkthrough of retrieval augmented generation—from a document entering the system to a grounded answer leaving it.</p>
    <div class="story-note"><span>Reading time · 6 minutes</span><span>All interactions · simulated locally</span></div>
  </header>

  <section class="story-section opening-section">
    <div class="section-number">01</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE PROBLEM</span><h3>A language model can sound certain about information it does not have.</h3><p>Imagine asking an assistant about a company policy written yesterday. The model may know how to write an answer, but it cannot reliably know that private or recently changed policy unless the system gives it the relevant source.</p></div>
    <div class="problem-demo"><div class="problem-column"><span class="mini-label">WITHOUT RETRIEVAL</span><p>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?”</p><div class="answer bad">I think it is escalated according to the usual process…<b>Confidence without evidence.</b></div></div><div class="problem-arrow">→</div><div class="problem-column"><span class="mini-label">WITH RETRIEVAL</span><p>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?”</p><div class="answer good">After the first review, route it to the regional operations queue and record a reason code.<b>Answer + source pointer.</b></div></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section core-section">
    <div class="section-number">02</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE CORE IDEA</span><h3>RAG is three moves: retrieve, augment, generate.</h3><p>The system searches a prepared knowledge base, places the best passages beside the question, and asks the model to answer within that evidence boundary.</p></div>
    <div class="core-diagram"><div class="core-card"><span>01</span><b>Retrieve</b><small>Find relevant passages</small></div><i>+</i><div class="core-card"><span>02</span><b>Augment</b><small>Add passages to the prompt</small></div><i>+</i><div class="core-card"><span>03</span><b>Generate</b><small>Answer with citations</small></div></div>
    <div class="takeaway">RAG does not retrain the model. It gives the model better material to work with at answer time.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section index-section">
    <div class="section-number">03</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">BEFORE A QUESTION ARRIVES</span><h3>First, make the knowledge searchable.</h3><p>This is the indexing path. It usually runs when documents are added or refreshed, not every time someone asks a question.</p></div>
    <div class="index-story"><div class="index-steps"><button class="index-step active" data-index-step="ingest"><span>1</span><b>Ingest</b><small>Parse files and preserve their source.</small></button><button class="index-step" data-index-step="chunk"><span>2</span><b>Chunk</b><small>Split documents into complete ideas.</small></button><button class="index-step" data-index-step="embed"><span>3</span><b>Embed</b><small>Represent meaning as vectors.</small></button><button class="index-step" data-index-step="store"><span>4</span><b>Index</b><small>Store vectors, terms, and metadata.</small></button></div><div class="index-explanation"><span class="story-kicker" data-index-kicker>INGEST</span><h4 data-index-title>Keep the source attached to the text.</h4><p data-index-copy>Documents can come from object storage, APIs, databases, wikis, or uploads. Parsing should preserve headings, tables, timestamps, and a pointer to the original source.</p><div class="index-visual" data-index-visual><span class="doc-badge">PDF</span><b>Payments operations handbook</b><small>42 pages · Finance / Analyst · updated 2 days ago</small></div></div></div>
    <div class="chunk-lab"><div><span class="story-kicker">SMALL EXPERIMENT</span><h4>Chunking is not just cutting text every 500 tokens.</h4><p>A useful chunk keeps one idea together. Click the options to see why boundaries affect retrieval quality.</p></div><div class="chunk-controls"><button class="chunk-button active" data-chunk="good">Complete idea</button><button class="chunk-button" data-chunk="bad">Cut mid-sentence</button><div class="chunk-panel" data-chunk-panel="good"><span>chunk_018 · heading: escalation</span><p>“If a payment exception remains unresolved after the first review, route it to the regional operations queue and record the reason code.”</p><em>Good retrieval unit · source preserved</em></div><div class="chunk-panel hidden" data-chunk-panel="bad"><span>chunk_018 · arbitrary page split</span><p>“If a payment exception remains unresolved after the first review, route it to the regional operations”</p><em class="warning-text">The text is findable, but the rule is incomplete.</em></div></div></div>
    <div class="concept-footnote"><b>In practice:</b> embeddings enable semantic search; sparse indexes preserve exact terms and identifiers; metadata enables filtering by source, tenant, freshness, or access scope. PostgreSQL with pgvector is one way to keep vectors close to application data.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section query-section">
    <div class="section-number">04</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">WHEN A QUESTION ARRIVES</span><h3>Search is a small pipeline, not a single similarity lookup.</h3><p>The system needs to understand the question, find enough candidates, remove what the user cannot see, then choose the evidence most likely to support an answer.</p></div>
    <div class="query-story"><div class="query-timeline"><button class="query-step active" data-query-step="rewrite"><b>01</b><span>Rewrite</span><small>Clarify intent</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="filter"><b>02</b><span>Filter</span><small>Apply permissions</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="retrieve"><b>03</b><span>Retrieve</span><small>Dense + sparse search</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="rerank"><b>04</b><span>Rerank</span><small>Order evidence</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="answer"><b>05</b><span>Answer</span><small>Cite or abstain</small></button></div><div class="query-explanation"><span class="story-kicker" data-query-kicker>QUERY REWRITE</span><h4 data-query-title>Make the user’s wording searchable.</h4><p data-query-copy>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?” becomes a clearer retrieval intent: payment exception, unresolved, escalation rule.</p><div class="query-signal" data-query-signal>payment exception · unresolved · escalation rule</div></div></div>
    <div class="retrieval-lab"><div class="lab-heading"><div><span class="story-kicker">SMALL EXPERIMENT</span><h4>Why combine retrieval methods?</h4></div><div class="retrieval-tabs"><button class="retrieval-tab active" data-retrieval="hybrid">Hybrid</button><button class="retrieval-tab" data-retrieval="semantic">Semantic</button><button class="retrieval-tab" data-retrieval="keyword">Keyword</button><button class="retrieval-tab" data-retrieval="reranked">Reranked</button></div></div><div class="retrieval-table"><div class="retrieval-row best"><span>01</span><div><b>Escalation policy · section 4.2</b><small>Exact rule and matching intent · Finance / Analyst</small></div><strong>0.94</strong></div><div class="retrieval-row"><span>02</span><div><b>Exception review checklist</b><small>Related process, weaker answer signal</small></div><strong>0.71</strong></div><div class="retrieval-row restricted"><span>03</span><div><b>Regional queue ownership notes</b><small>Relevant wording, blocked by access filter</small></div><strong>0.68</strong></div></div><p class="lab-note" data-retrieval-note>Hybrid retrieval catches both meaning and exact business language. Reranking then spends more compute on a smaller, stronger set.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section answer-section">
    <div class="section-number">05</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE EVIDENCE BOUNDARY</span><h3>The model should see a small, relevant context—not the whole database.</h3><p>After ranking, the system packs the best passages into a context window. It keeps their source pointers, applies the user’s question, and asks the model to stay faithful to what was retrieved.</p></div>
    <div class="answer-demo"><div class="answer-text"><span class="story-kicker">GROUNDED RESPONSE</span><p>After the first review, an unresolved payment exception is routed to the regional operations queue and recorded with a reason code.</p></div><div class="citation"><b>Source</b><span>Payments operations handbook · §4.2</span><em>Supported by retained evidence</em></div></div>
    <div class="takeaway">When evidence is weak, the most useful answer may be: “I could not find enough support for that. Can you narrow the question or provide another source?”</div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section quality-section">
    <div class="section-number">06</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE QUALITY LOOP</span><h3>RAG is only useful when the whole path is measured.</h3><p>A fluent answer can still be wrong. Teams evaluate retrieval and generation separately, then watch latency, cost, freshness, access decisions, and traces in production.</p></div>
    <div class="metrics"><div><b>Recall@5</b><strong>0.92</strong><small>Did we retrieve the relevant chunk?</small></div><div><b>Context precision</b><strong>0.86</strong><small>How much retrieved context was useful?</small></div><div><b>Faithfulness</b><strong>0.96</strong><small>Did the answer follow its evidence?</small></div><div><b>Relevancy</b><strong>0.89</strong><small>Did it answer the question?</small></div></div>
    <div class="failure-lab"><div><span class="story-kicker">FAILURE MODE LAB</span><h4>What should the system do when things go wrong?</h4><div class="failure-buttons"><button class="failure-button active" data-failure="miss">Missed evidence</button><button class="failure-button" data-failure="stale">Stale source</button><button class="failure-button" data-failure="access">Access conflict</button><button class="failure-button" data-failure="unsupported">Unsupported claim</button></div></div><div class="failure-result"><b data-failure-title>Recall fell because the relevant chunk never entered the candidate set.</b><p data-failure-copy>Improve parsing, query rewriting, chunk boundaries, or retrieval coverage. Do not ask the generator to guess.</p><span data-failure-action>Next move: inspect retrieval recall and add a representative test question.</span></div></div>
  </section>

  <footer class="story-close"><span class="story-kicker">THE SHORT VERSION</span><h3>RAG is a system for giving a model the right context—and proving where the answer came from.</h3><div class="close-pipeline"><span>documents</span><i>→</i><span>chunks</span><i>→</i><span>retrieval</span><i>→</i><span>evidence</span><i>→</i><span>grounded answer</span></div><p>This demo uses deterministic public fixtures. It explains the architecture without exposing private documents, calling a live model, or pretending that a static page is production telemetry.</p></footer>
</article>

